  "theorem_statement": "Let k be algebraically closed of characteristic 0, let M_{6,6} be the moduli stack of smooth proper genus-6 curves with six ordered distinct marked points (smooth Deligne-Mumford, dim 21), let pi : C -> M_{6,6} be the universal curve with tautological sections sigma_1,...,sigma_6 and universal marking divisor Gamma = sigma_1 + ... + sigma_6 (a relative effective Cartier divisor of degree 6, etale over the base). Define the closed substack Z := V(Fitt_1(R^1 pi_* O_C(Gamma))) inside M_{6,6}. Then: (Z1) Z = V(Fitt_2(R^1 pi_*(omega_{C/M}(-Gamma)))) = V(I_{k-2}(phi)) for every 2-term complex phi : K^0 -> K^1 of vector bundles (ranks k, k-1) universally computing R pi_* O_C(Gamma); the formation of Z commutes with arbitrary base change; Z is S_6-stable and closed. (Z2) A geometric point (C,p_1,...,p_6) lies in Z iff h^0(C,O_C(Gamma)) >= 3, iff h^0(C,omega_C(-Gamma)) >= 2, iff omega_C(-Gamma) is a (possibly base-point-bearing) g^1_4 residual to Gamma in the canonical series; for C non-hyperelliptic these are equivalent to h^0(O_C(Gamma)) = 3, h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) = 2, and to the six canonical images spanning at most a P^3 in P^5. (Z3) Under the Casnati-Ekedahl normalization O_{P(E^vee)}(1)|_C = omega_C tensor gamma^* O_{P^1}(2) (hypothesis H1) and the packet balanced splitting F = O_{P^1}(4) + O_{P^1}(5) (H2), for every base-point-free degree-4 morphism gamma : C -> P^1 with tetragonal divisor D_gamma one has W_4|_C = omega_C^{tensor 2} and W_5|_C = omega_C^{tensor 2}(-D_gamma); H^0(C, omega_C tensor W_4^vee(Gamma)) = 0 for every degree-6 divisor Gamma; H^0(C, omega_C tensor W_5^vee(Gamma)) is nonzero iff O_C(Gamma) = W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} = omega_C(-D_gamma), i.e. iff Gamma + D_gamma is a canonical divisor; consequently the image in M_{6,6} of the balanced Casnati-Ekedahl evaluation-failure locus is exactly the OPEN substack Z^{bal} of Z consisting of points at which omega_C(-Gamma) carries a base-point-free pencil with balanced CE splitting, so that the balanced evaluation-failure image is not closed in M_{6,6} whereas Z is. (Z4) Z is nonempty, unconditionally on H1/H2: a general complete intersection C of divisors of bidegrees (1,2) and (2,2) in P^1 x P^2 is a smooth connected curve of genus 6 with omega_C = O_C(1,1), with gamma = pr_1|_C finite of degree 4, D_gamma = O_C(1,0), and omega_C(-D_gamma) = O_C(0,1) base-point-free of degree 6 with h^0 = 3; a general member of |O_C(0,1)| is reduced with six distinct points, and the resulting marked curve lies in Z; moreover for this C the CE data are E^vee = O_{P^1}(-3)^{+3} and F = O_{P^1}(4) + O_{P^1}(5), so the balanced hypothesis H2 is realized and Z^{bal} is nonempty. (Z5) The projection Z -> M_6 is surjective, and over the Petri-general locus its fibre is the disjoint union, over the five points L of W^2_6(C), of the ordered-distinct-support open of |L| = P^2; hence Z has a component dominating M_6 which is smooth of dimension 17, i.e. of codimension 4 in M_{6,6}. (Z6) The determinantal presentation of Z has expected codimension 6 while codim Z = 4, so Z is nowhere cut out by a regular sequence in that presentation and carries excess dimension 2; any localization or Gysin argument downstream must therefore use a genuine stratification rather than a codimension-4 (or codimension-6) regular-embedding Gysin map. (Z7) Z -> [Z/S_6] is a finite etale S_6-torsor of algebraic stacks and [Z/S_6] is canonically the reduced-support open substack of the relative degree-six effective-divisor incidence stack; the analogous assertion on coarse moduli spaces is false, since curves with automorphisms give nontrivial stabilizers.",
  "theorem_scope": {"g": 6, "n": 6},
  "summary": "Constructs the evaluation-failure locus Z as a canonical, base-change-compatible CLOSED substack of M_{6,6} defined by a Fitting ideal of R^1 pi_* O(Gamma), and identifies it intrinsically as the Brill-Noether locus {h^0(O_C(Gamma)) >= 3} = {h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) >= 2}, i.e. the locus where the six markings are residual to a tetragonal divisor in the canonical series. Under the CE normalization plus the packet balanced splitting, the balanced Casnati-Ekedahl evaluation-failure condition O_C(Gamma) = W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} is proved equivalent to Gamma + D_gamma being canonical, so the balanced failure image is an OPEN substack of Z. Nonemptiness is proved unconditionally by an explicit balanced (1,2)-(2,2) complete intersection in P^1 x P^2 (re-proving FND-0005). The dominant component is smooth of dimension 17, codimension 4, but the determinantal presentation has expected codimension 6: Z carries excess dimension 2, which invalidates naive regular-embedding Gysin bookkeeping downstream. Does not resolve the exact target.",
  "argument_markdown": "## 0. Scope, and what this attempt does and does not do\n\nThis attempt discharges the assigned subproblem `C66-GEO-Z` (\"Construct and identify the evaluation-failure locus Z\") only. It does **not** claim the exact theorem of the campaign (`RED-0001`), and it explicitly does not attempt `C66-GEO-H0`, `C66-GEO-COMP`, `C66-TATE-SUPPORT`, `C66-TATE-ASSEMBLY`, or either counterexample subproblem. `TASK.json` lists `blocked_dependencies: []` and `dependency_artifacts: {}`, so no dependency attempt or confirmation review was supplied; accordingly no dependency artifact IDs are cited and no absent dependency is inferred, recreated, or strengthened.\n\nAll working-context files supplied are treated as ordinary context. Every fact used below is reproved here from first principles, from the packet's adjudicated findings, or from explicitly labelled classical inputs.\n\nBase field: algebraically closed, characteristic 0. All cohomology of coherent sheaves; \"stack\" means algebraic (Deligne-Mumford) stack.\n\n### 0.1 Blocked routes\n\nNone of the four blocked routes is used, and `new_inputs` is empty:\n\n- `vcd-only-vanishing`: no vanishing-above-vcd statement occurs anywhere below. The packet's FND-0035 is quoted only to record that such vanishing is inert at (6,6); it is not used as a premise.\n- `undecorated-cgp-top-weight-graph-complex`: no graph complex, dual complex, or top-weight combinatorial model appears.\n- `point-count-without-degree-parity-separation`: no point counts over finite fields are used. The only finite objects appearing (five `g^1_4`'s, the `S_6`-cover) are used as geometric statements over an algebraically closed field of characteristic 0, not as counting evidence.\n- `forgetful-map-renaming-local-system-unknown`: the forgetful map `M_{6,6} -> M_{6,5}` and the Leray reduction of FND-0036 are deliberately **not** used. Nothing below renames the unknown as a cohomology group with unknown local-system coefficients.\n\n## 1. Setup and notation\n\nLet `M := M_{6,6}` be the moduli stack of `(C, p_1, ..., p_6)` with `C` smooth proper connected of genus 6 and `p_i` ordered and **pairwise distinct**. It is a smooth DM stack of dimension `3g - 3 + n = 21` (FND-0001's dimension bookkeeping; also direct: `3*6 - 3 + 6 = 21`).\n\nLet `pi : \\mathcal{C} -> M` be the universal curve, `sigma_1, ..., sigma_6` the tautological sections, and\n\n`Gamma := sigma_1(M) + ... + sigma_6(M) \\subset \\mathcal{C}`,\n\na relative effective Cartier divisor of degree 6 which is **etale over `M`** (the sections are disjoint by definition of `M_{6,6}`). Write `omega := omega_{\\mathcal{C}/M}`.\n\n**Observation 1.1 (reducedness is automatic).** On `M_{6,6}` the marking divisor `Gamma` is automatically reduced with six distinct support points, because distinctness of the `p_i` is part of the definition of the moduli problem. Consequently the \"non-reduced support\" and \"reduced-support open is not closed\" issues, which are genuine for the relative effective-divisor (Hilbert) space over tetragonal moduli, **do not arise for a locus defined inside `M_{6,6}`**. This is the structural reason why the construction below produces a closed substack of `M_{6,6}` rather than a merely locally closed one.\n\n## 2. Construction of `Z` (proved)\n\n### 2.1 The definition\n\n`R^1 pi_*` of a coherent sheaf flat over the base, for a proper flat morphism of relative dimension 1, is the top nonvanishing higher direct image; by Grothendieck's cohomology-and-base-change in top degree it commutes with **arbitrary** base change and its fibre at a geometric point `s` is `H^1(C_s, -)`. Fitting ideals of a coherent sheaf also commute with arbitrary base change, and `V(Fitt_j(\\mathcal{F}))` is exactly the locus where the fibre dimension of `\\mathcal{F}` exceeds `j` (Nakayama).\n\n**Definition 2.1.** `Z := V( Fitt_1( R^1 pi_* O_{\\mathcal{C}}(Gamma) ) ) \\subseteq M_{6,6}`.\n\n**Lemma 2.2 (canonicity, base change, closedness, `S_6`-stability).**\n\n1. `Z` is a closed substack of `M_{6,6}`.\n2. Formation of `Z` commutes with arbitrary base change `T -> M_{6,6}`.\n3. If `phi : K^0 -> K^1` is any complex of vector bundles on an open `U \\subseteq M_{6,6}` universally computing `R pi_* O(Gamma)` (such `phi` exist Zariski-locally by the standard Mumford construction), with `rk K^0 = k`, then `rk K^1 = k - 1` and `Z \\cap U = V(I_{k-2}(phi))`, the locus where `phi` has rank `<= k - 3`.\n4. `Z = V( Fitt_2( R^1 pi_*( omega(-Gamma) ) ) )`.\n5. `Z` is stable under the `S_6`-action permuting the sections.\n\n*Proof.* (1) `Fitt_1` is a coherent ideal sheaf; its vanishing locus is closed. (2) Both `R^1 pi_*` (top degree) and `Fitt_j` commute with arbitrary base change; hence so does the vanishing locus of the Fitting ideal.\n\n(3) `chi(O_{C_s}(Gamma_s)) = deg - g + 1 = 6 - 6 + 1 = 1`, so `rk K^0 - rk K^1 = 1`, i.e. `rk K^1 = k - 1`. With `\\mathcal{F} = coker(phi) = R^1 pi_* O(Gamma)` presented by `K^0 -> K^1 -> \\mathcal{F} -> 0`, the standard identity `Fitt_j(coker phi) = I_{rk K^1 - j}(phi)` gives `Fitt_1(\\mathcal{F}) = I_{(k-1)-1}(phi) = I_{k-2}(phi)`, whose vanishing locus is `{ rk phi <= k - 3 }`.\n\n(4) Grothendieck-Serre relative duality for the smooth proper family of curves `pi` gives `R pi_*( omega tensor \\mathcal{L}^{vee} ) \\cong ( R pi_* \\mathcal{L} )^{vee} [-1]` in the derived category. Taking `\\mathcal{L} = O(Gamma)` and representing `R pi_* O(Gamma)` by `phi : K^0 -> K^1`, the complex `phi^{vee} : (K^1)^{vee} -> (K^0)^{vee}` (ranks `k-1`, `k`) represents `R pi_*( omega(-Gamma) )`. Hence `R^1 pi_*( omega(-Gamma) ) = coker(phi^{vee})` and `Fitt_2( coker phi^{vee} ) = I_{k-2}(phi^{vee}) = I_{k-2}(phi)`, the identical minor ideal. So the two Fitting definitions define literally the same ideal sheaf. (Consistency check on fibres: `h^1(omega(-Gamma)) = h^0(O(Gamma))` by Serre duality, so `V(Fitt_2)` is `{ h^0(O(Gamma)) >= 3 }`, matching `V(Fitt_1(R^1 pi_* O(Gamma))) = { h^1(O(Gamma)) >= 2 } = { h^0(O(Gamma)) >= 3 }` via `h^0 - h^1 = 1`.)\n\n(5) The defining ideal depends only on the divisor `Gamma`, which is `S_6`-invariant. `\\square`\n\n**Remark 2.3 (what this avoids).** Definition 2.1 uses neither `Proj(pi_* L)` nor a relative linear system over a base on which `h^0` jumps, and it needs no comparison of an Abel-Jacobi incidence scheme with a determinantal scheme. The two potential pathologies flagged in the working contexts - (a) `pi_* L` not locally free / not commuting with base change when `h^0(L)` jumps, (b) geometric-point agreement not determining nilpotents - are both circumvented because the construction only ever pushes forward in **top** degree, where base change is unconditional, and because the Fitting ideal is intrinsic. See GAP-2 for the comparison that is genuinely left open.\n\n## 3. Identification of `Z` (proved, intrinsic)\n\n**Proposition 3.1.** Let `(C, p_1, ..., p_6)` be a geometric point of `M_{6,6}`, `Gamma = p_1 + ... + p_6`. The following are equivalent.\n\n(a) `(C, p_\\bullet) \\in Z`.\n(b) `h^0(C, O_C(Gamma)) >= 3`.\n(c) `h^0(C, omega_C(-Gamma)) >= 2`.\n(d) There is a degree-4 line bundle `D` on `C` with `h^0(D) >= 2` and `Gamma + D \\in |omega_C|`.\n\nMoreover, if `C` is non-hyperelliptic then in (b) and (c) equality holds: `h^0(O_C(Gamma)) = 3` and `h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) = 2`, `omega_C(-Gamma)` is a `g^1_4`, and (a)-(d) are further equivalent to:\n\n(e) the six points span at most a `P^3` under the canonical embedding `C \\hookrightarrow P^5`.\n\n*Proof.* (a) `<=>` (b) is Lemma 2.2(3)-(4) read on fibres. (b) `<=>` (c): Riemann-Roch gives `h^0(O(Gamma)) - h^1(O(Gamma)) = 6 - 6 + 1 = 1`, and Serre duality gives `h^1(O(Gamma)) = h^0(omega_C(-Gamma))`; hence `h^0(O(Gamma)) = h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) + 1`. (c) `=>` (d): take `D := omega_C(-Gamma)`, of degree `10 - 6 = 4`, with `h^0(D) >= 2`; then `Gamma + D` is a canonical divisor class and, being of the right degree and effective in the required sense, `D` is effective, so `Gamma + D \\in |omega_C|`. (d) `=>` (c): `Gamma + D \\in |omega_C|` forces `D \\cong omega_C(-Gamma)`, so `h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) = h^0(D) >= 2`.\n\nBound: on `Z` we have `h^1(O(Gamma)) >= 2 > 0`, so `O(Gamma)` is special, and Clifford's theorem gives `h^0(O(Gamma)) - 1 <= deg/2 = 3`, i.e. `h^0 <= 4`, with equality only if `O(Gamma)` is trivial, canonical, or `C` is hyperelliptic. Since `deg Gamma = 6 \\ne 0, 10`, equality forces `C` hyperelliptic. Hence for non-hyperelliptic `C`, `h^0(O(Gamma)) = 3` and `h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) = 2` exactly, so `omega_C(-Gamma)` is a `g^1_4`.\n\n(e): geometric Riemann-Roch gives `dim span(Gamma) = deg Gamma - 1 - dim|Gamma| = 6 - 1 - (h^0(O(Gamma)) - 1) = 6 - h^0(O(Gamma))`. Thus `h^0 = 1` gives a spanning `P^5`, and `h^0 >= 3` gives span `<= P^3`. `\\square`\n\n**Interpretation.** `Z` is exactly the locus where the six marked points **fail by 2 to impose independent conditions on the canonical system**, equivalently where `Gamma` moves in a net `g^2_6`, equivalently where `Gamma` is residual, in a canonical divisor, to a degree-4 pencil. This identification uses no Casnati-Ekedahl convention, no splitting type, and no choice of tetragonal structure: it is intrinsic to `(C, Gamma)`.\n\n## 4. The bridge to the Casnati-Ekedahl evaluation failure\n\n### 4.1 Source-backed inputs and the one normalization hypothesis\n\n**(S1)** [SRC-0004, Lemma 10.6, packet primary locator] For `i = 1, 2`, with `W_{f_i} = (O_{P(E^vee)}(2) tensor gamma^* O_{P^1}(-f_i))|_C`, `deg(W_{f_i}|_C) = 2(2g - 2 + 4*2) - 4 f_i = 4g + 12 - 4 f_i`; the obstruction splits as `H^0(C, omega_C tensor W^vee(Gamma)) = H^0(omega_C tensor W_{f_1}^vee(Gamma)) + H^0(omega_C tensor W_{f_2}^vee(Gamma))` and `deg(omega_C tensor W_{f_i}^vee(Gamma)) = 4 f_i + n - 2g - 14`.\n\n**(S2)** [packet canonical geometric bottleneck] Balanced splitting `F = O_{P^1}(4) + O_{P^1}(5)`; `W_5 := (O_{P(E^vee)}(2) tensor gamma^* O_{P^1}(-5))|_C` is the `f_2 = 5` summand of `(O(2) tensor gamma^* F^vee)|_C`, **not** the `O_{P^1}(5)` summand of `F`; the six-point failure condition is `O_C(Gamma) \\cong W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1}`.\n\n**(H1) CE normalization hypothesis.** `O_{P(E^vee)}(1)|_C \\cong omega_{C/P^1} = omega_C tensor gamma^* O_{P^1}(2)`.\n\nH1 is the standard Casnati-Ekedahl normalization of the relative canonical embedding, and it is *forced numerically* by (S1): (S1) computes `deg(O_{P(E^vee)}(2)|_C) = 2(2g - 2 + 8)`, so `deg(O_{P(E^vee)}(1)|_C) = 2g - 2 + 8 = deg(omega_{C/P^1})`. Degree agreement does not by itself prove isomorphism, so H1 is recorded as a **source-backed input/hypothesis**, not as a proved step. It is verified directly in the explicit example of Section 5. **Everything in Sections 2, 3, 5(nonemptiness of `Z`), 6 and 7 is independent of H1**; only Section 4.2-4.3 uses it.\n\n**(H2)** Balanced splitting, as in (S2). Section 4.3 records precisely what fails without H2.\n\n### 4.2 The failure condition under H1 + H2 (proved from S1, S2, H1)\n\n**Lemma 4.1.** Assume H1 and let `gamma : C -> P^1` be finite of degree 4 with tetragonal divisor `D_gamma := gamma^*(pt)` (degree 4). Then for every `f`,\n\n`W_f|_C \\cong omega_C^{tensor 2} tensor gamma^* O_{P^1}(4 - f)`.\n\nIn particular, with `g = 6` and H2: `W_4|_C \\cong omega_C^{tensor 2}` (degree 20) and `W_5|_C \\cong omega_C^{tensor 2}(-D_gamma)` (degree 16), and\n\n`W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} \\cong omega_C(-D_gamma)`, of degree `10 - 4 = 6`.\n\n*Proof.* Square H1: `O_{P(E^vee)}(2)|_C \\cong omega_C^{tensor 2} tensor gamma^* O(4)`; tensor with `gamma^* O(-f)`. Degrees: `deg gamma^* O(1)|_C = 4`, so `deg W_f = 2(2g - 2) + 4(4 - f) = 4g + 12 - 4f`, matching (S1) exactly; at `g = 6`, `f = 5` this is 16, and `deg(W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1}) = 16 - 10 = 6`, matching the packet degree calculation. `\\square`\n\n**Lemma 4.2 (the two obstruction summands).** Let `g = 6`, `n = 6`, `Gamma` any effective divisor of degree 6 on `C`, `gamma` as above with balanced splitting (H2).\n\n1. `deg(omega_C tensor W_4^vee(Gamma)) = 4*4 + 6 - 12 - 14 = -4 < 0`, hence `H^0(C, omega_C tensor W_4^vee(Gamma)) = 0` **always**. (A negative-degree line bundle on a smooth projective connected curve has no nonzero global section.)\n2. `deg(omega_C tensor W_5^vee(Gamma)) = 4*5 + 6 - 12 - 14 = 0`, and a degree-zero line bundle on a smooth projective connected curve has a nonzero section iff it is trivial. Hence `H^0(C, omega_C tensor W_5^vee(Gamma)) \\ne 0` iff `O_C(Gamma) \\cong W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1}`.\n3. Under H1, `omega_C tensor W_5^vee(Gamma) \\cong omega_C^{-1}(D_gamma + Gamma)`, so the failure condition reads `Gamma + D_gamma \\in |omega_C|`.\n\n*Proof.* The degrees are (S1) evaluated at `(g, n) = (6, 6)`; item 3 substitutes Lemma 4.1. Note that item 2 is independent of H1 (it needs only `deg W_5 = 16` from (S1)); item 3 needs H1. `\\square`\n\nThis is the derivation of the failure condition \"from the `f_2 = 5` summand in SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6, including the sign in `gamma^* O(-5)`\" required by the packet. The sign matters: with `gamma^* O(+5)` one would get `deg W_5 = 4g + 12 + 20 = 56` and the obstruction degree `10 - 56 + 6 = -40 < 0`, giving unconditional vanishing and an empty locus; the packet's `gamma^* O(-5)` is what makes the obstruction degree exactly 0.\n\n### 4.3 Where the balanced failure locus sits inside `Z`\n\n**Proposition 4.3.** Assume H1. Let `(C, p_\\bullet, gamma)` be a marked genus-6 curve together with a base-point-free degree-4 morphism `gamma` whose CE splitting is balanced. If CE evaluation fails at `(C, gamma, Gamma)`, then `(C, p_\\bullet) \\in Z`. Conversely, `(C, p_\\bullet) \\in Z` arises from such a triple iff the degree-4 bundle `omega_C(-"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3329,"cache_read_input_tokens":48468,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3329},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"co